<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Latin Cherubikon of the Missa greca (Essen, Corvey and Saint-Denis)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">late 10th century (manuscripts)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation and transliteration; prototype of the Western offertorium</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cherouvikon asmatikon (cathedral rite of Hagia Sophia)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">13th–15th centuries (manuscripts)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">composed choral settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cherubikon palatinon, longer elaboration attributed to John Koukouzeles</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elaboration</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cycle of eight cherubika (Manuel Chrysaphes)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1458</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new settings in the eight modes</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cheruvimskaya Pesn (Grigory L'vovsky)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Church Slavonic setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cheruvimskaya Pesn (Alexander Kastalsky)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Church Slavonic setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cherouvikon in the plagal first mode (Thrasyvoulos Stanitsas)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1961</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new setting</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sanctus of the Anaphora, from Isaiah 6:1–3</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the angels' thrice-holy song the text refers to (or the 5th-century Trisagion)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Homily on Isaiah 6 (John Chrysostom)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">late 4th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the idea of the liturgy's chant joining the choirs of angels</span></li></ul></section>
